<article class="env-definition" id="04JJ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04JJ">Definition <span data-tag="04JJ">58.2.1</span>.</a> Let $G$ be a topological group. A <em>$G$-set</em>, sometimes called a <em>discrete $G$-set</em>, is a set $X$ endowed with a left action $a : G \times X \to X$ such that $a$ is continuous when $X$ is given the discrete topology and $G \times X$ the product topology. A <em>morphism of $G$-sets</em> $f : X \to Y$ is simply any $G$-equivariant map from $X$ to $Y$. The category of $G$-sets is denoted <em>$G\textit{-Sets}$</em>. </p>
</article>